  "Number": 7,
  "Question": "A town’s population increased by \\(1{,}200\\) people, and then this new population decreased by \\(11\\%.\\) The town now had \\(32\\) less people than it did before the \\(1{,}200\\) increase. What is the original population?",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(1{,}200\\)",
    "B": "\\(11{,}200\\)",
    "C": "\\(9{,}968\\)",
    "D": "\\(10{,}000\\)",
    "E": "none of these"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "D",
  "Solution": "The decrease must undo the increase of \\(1{,}200\\) people and remove another \\(32\\) people. Thus the number lost was\n\\[\n1{,}200+32=1{,}232.\n\\]\nThis was \\(11\\%\\) of the population immediately after the increase. Therefore that population was\n\\[\n\\frac{1{,}232}{0.11}=11{,}200.\n\\]\nSubtracting the earlier increase gives the original population:\n\\[\n11{,}200-1{,}200=\\boxed{10{,}000},\n\\]\nchoice \\(\\mathrm{D}\\).",
